from common import topic

topic('N1',[
('What does a decision problem ask for?','A yes/no answer',['An optimum value necessarily','A list of every solution','A running-time graph'],'The output is membership/feasibility rather than a best value or all witnesses.'),
('Which is a TSP decision question?','Does a tour of cost at most K exist?',['What is the cheapest tour?','List every tour','Sort all edge weights'],'The threshold version has a yes/no answer.'),
('For standard three-peg Hanoi, may a larger disk rest on a smaller disk?','No',['Yes for one move','Yes on the destination peg','Only if disks have equal labels'],'The size-order rule holds on every peg throughout the trace.'),
('Does one slow known algorithm prove every algorithm for the problem must be slow?','No',['Yes by definition','Yes if it uses recursion','Only if it is deterministic'],'An upper-bound implementation is not a lower bound over all algorithms.'),
('How many moves does the standard optimal three-disk Hanoi transfer need?','7',['6','8','3'],'M(3)=2M(2)+1=2·3+1=7.'),
('For M(1)=1 and M(n)=2M(n−1)+1, what exact solution follows?','2ⁿ−1',['n²','2n−1','n!'],'Substitution gives2(2^(n−1)−1)+1=2ⁿ−1 and the base fits.'),
('Why does explicitly listing all 2ⁿ binary strings of length n require exponential output work?','There are exponentially many output objects',['Every individual string is infinite','No algorithm can generate any of them','The alphabet is unbounded'],'Even one operation per output already costs Ω(2ⁿ), with writing all characters costing more.'),
('How can an exact optimisation solver answer its threshold decision version?','Compute the optimum and compare it with K',['Ignore K','Always return yes','Sort K as an edge'],'For minimisation, optimum≤K exactly when a feasible solution meeting K exists, with infeasibility handled.'),
('Why must the task and representation be specified before discussing hardness?','Decision, optimisation and explicit enumeration can have different outputs and input measures',['All representations have equal length','All hard tasks are identical','Only source-code length matters'],'Complexity is measured against a defined input encoding and required output.'),
('Does an exponential-size explicit output imply its related yes/no decision problem needs exponential time?','Not by itself',['Yes automatically','Yes for every enumeration task','Only if there are duplicate outputs'],'The decision output is one bit; an enumeration output lower bound does not automatically transfer.'),
('Why does standard Hanoi force moving all n−1 smaller disks away before the largest disk first reaches the destination?','They must clear its source and cannot block its destination',['The largest disk can jump over them without constraints','All smaller disks are deleted','Every peg must remain nonempty'],'Under three pegs, this clearing requires transferring the smaller stack to the remaining peg, then restoring it on the destination.'),
('An exponential exhaustive search is shown for TSP. What conclusion is warranted?','That search has exponential cost; no universal lower bound follows from it alone',['No polynomial algorithm can exist by that fact alone','TSP has no finite algorithm','Every graph lacks a tour'],'A poor or exhaustive method does not prove optimal complexity over all possible algorithms.'),
('Malfoid equates “computationally hard” with “I found this trace confusing.” What should replace that claim?','A precise task, input-size measure and justified complexity argument',['A longer variable name','A single slow laptop run','A claim that recursion is impossible'],'Informal difficulty is not a computational complexity proof.')])

topic('N2',[
('What kind of problems are formally classified by P and NP here?','Decision problems',['Only sorting functions','Only optimisation outputs','All unencoded physical tasks'],'The formal definitions concern yes/no membership languages.'),
('What does P require?','A deterministic polynomial-time decision algorithm',['An exponential-time verifier','A short program regardless of runtime','Only fast yes answers without no answers'],'A decider must correctly answer both yes and no within a polynomial input-length bound.'),
('What does NP verification require for yes instances?','A polynomial-length certificate checked by a polynomial-time verifier',['A certificate for every no instance under the same definition','A superpolynomial certificate always','A random guess accepted without checking'],'The witness length and checking time are both bounded polynomially in the encoded instance size.'),
('Which inclusion is known from the definitions?','P⊆NP',['NP⊆P is established by definition','P and NP are disjoint','P contains no decision problem'],'A polynomial decider can serve as a verifier that ignores the certificate.'),
('What does NP stand for?','Nondeterministic polynomial time',['Non-polynomial time','No possible program','Numerically polynomial time'],'NP is not defined as the set of problems outside polynomial time.'),
('A TSP tour certificate lists a permutation of vertices. What must a verifier check beyond its cost?','Every vertex appears exactly once and the closing tour is valid',['Only the first edge','Only that all labels are integers','Only the largest edge'],'The certificate must meet the full feasibility predicate, including returning to the start.'),
('Why does rejecting one proposed certificate not prove an NP instance is no?','Another certificate may be accepted',['Verifiers can never reject','Every instance is yes','Certificates must all be identical'],'Yes means there exists a valid witness; one failed candidate does not negate that existential claim.'),
('Does easy verification automatically give an easy deterministic search over all certificates?','No; the candidate space may be exponential',['Yes; try every certificate in polynomial time','Yes because the verifier ignores input','Only for NP-complete problems'],'Polynomial witness length permits exponentially many possible witnesses; checking one is different from finding one.'),
('A verifier accepts every input on certificate0. Why is it invalid for a nontrivial language with no instances?','It lacks soundness: no instances receive an accepted certificate',['Its certificate is too short','It is too fast','It must use randomness'],'For a no instance, every certificate must be rejected.'),
('A verifier rejects all certificates for one actual yes instance. Which requirement fails?','Completeness',['Soundness only','Polynomial runtime necessarily','Certificate encoding necessarily'],'Every yes instance must have at least one accepted witness.'),
('If certificates of length polynomial in |x| are allowed but checking them can take 2^|x| time, is NP membership proved?','No; polynomial verification time is also required',['Yes; witness length alone suffices','Yes if the verifier eventually halts','Only if certificates contain numbers'],'Both bounds are required, measured against the encoded input size with a polynomially bounded witness.'),
('What would a proof that one language in NP is outside P establish?','P≠NP',['P=NP','NP is empty','Every NP problem is outside P'],'Such a language would separate the classes, since P⊆NP.'),
('Malfoid says NP problems have no polynomial algorithm because NP means non-polynomial. What is wrong?','NP includes P, and the abbreviation has a different meaning',['P⊆NP is false','Every NP problem is known exponential','NP excludes easy verification'],'The definition is nondeterministic polynomial time; it does not assert deterministic hardness of every member.')])

topic('N3',[
('What does A≤pB mean in the many-one model used here?','A instances map in polynomial time to equivalent B instances',['B must map to A','A is faster on every measured input','A and B have identical inputs'],'There is one polynomial function f with x∈A iff f(x)∈B.'),
('Given A≤pB and a polynomial-time solver for B, what follows?','A has a polynomial-time solver',['B must be harder than every language','A is outside NP','A requires exponential time'],'Compute f(x), run the B solver and return its yes/no answer.'),
('To prove a proposed target B hard using known-hard A, which direction is needed?','A≤pB',['B≤pA only','A≤pA only','B≤pB only'],'An efficient B algorithm must then solve the known-hard source A.'),
('What logical relationship must a many-one reduction preserve?','Yes iff yes',['Yes implies yes only','Only equal instance sizes','Equal optimum values always'],'Both directions are necessary to preserve no instances as well.'),
('If A≤pB and B≤pC, what follows?','A≤pC',['C≤pA necessarily','A=C as sets','B has no algorithm'],'Compose the polynomial transformations; output size is also polynomially bounded.'),
('If A≤pB and A is easy, does that prove B easy?','No',['Yes','Only when A is nonempty','Only when f is deterministic'],'An easy source may reduce to a much harder target; the solver implication goes from target to source.'),
('If A≤pB and B∈P, which result follows?','A∈P',['A∉P','B is NP-complete automatically','P≠NP'],'Polynomial transformation followed by polynomial solving remains polynomial.'),
('If A≤pB and A∉P, what can be concluded about B?','B∉P',['B∈P','A and B must have equal runtime','B is undecidable necessarily'],'Otherwise a polynomial B solver would yield a polynomial A solver, contradicting A∉P.'),
('A construction proves only x∈A⇒f(x)∈B. What is missing?','Show f(x)∈B⇒x∈A',['Prove the function has a shorter name','Prove every B instance is an output','Show f(x)=x always'],'Without the reverse implication, no instances could map to yes instances and the answer would be unsound.'),
('Why must reduction runtime include the size of its constructed instance?','Writing the output must also fit polynomial time',['Output length is irrelevant','A reduction may write infinitely many symbols','Only the source certificate is measured'],'A polynomial-time explicit construction cannot produce a superpolynomial-length output.'),
('Does an algorithm using several adaptive calls to B automatically constitute one many-one reduction A≤pB?','No; that is a different reduction model unless converted',['Yes by definition','Only if it uses recursion','No reduction may use any solver conceptually'],'A many-one proof needs a single transformed instance with an iff condition; an oracle-use illustration alone is insufficient.'),
('A known NP-complete problem A reduces to B, but B∈NP has not been proved. What is still missing before calling B NP-complete?','NP membership',['The wrong-way reduction B≤pA instead','A timing benchmark only','A proof that B has no algorithm'],'Hardness and membership are separate obligations.'),
('Malfoid proves B≤pA for known-hard A and calls B hard. What is the correction?','That direction shows B is no harder than A; hardness needs A≤pB',['Reverse implication is never needed','Any arrow involving A proves B hard','B must equal A'],'A solver for A can solve B under the given direction, which does not establish difficulty of B.')])

topic('N4',[
('In the taught HP→TSP construction on n≥3 vertices, what weight is assigned to an original graph edge?','1',['0','2','n'],'Original adjacency is encoded by the cheaper complete-graph edge.'),
('What weight is assigned to a pair that was not an edge in the original graph?','2',['0','1','Infinity'],'The target is a complete graph with every pair assigned weight1 or2.'),
('What decision threshold is used?','n+1',['n−1','2n','1'],'An original Hamiltonian path uses n−1 cheap edges and can close using at most one weight-two edge.'),
('How many edges are in a Hamiltonian cycle on n≥3 vertices?','n',['n−1','n+1','2n'],'Each of n vertices contributes one edge to its successor in the cyclic order.'),
('If the original Hamiltonian path has n−1 edges of weight1, what is the maximum constructed cost after closing it?','n+1',['n−1','2n','n+2'],'The closing edge costs at most2, so(n−1)+2=n+1.'),
('A target tour has r weight-two edges and n−r weight-one edges. What is its cost?','n+r',['n−r','2n+r','r only'],'Sum2r+(n−r)=n+r.'),
('At threshold n+1, how many weight-two edges can an accepted tour contain?','At most one',['At most two','Any number','Exactly n−1'],'n+r≤n+1 implies r≤1.'),
('If an accepted tour has exactly one weight-two edge, how is an original Hamiltonian path obtained?','Delete that weight-two edge',['Delete all vertices touching it','Keep it and delete every weight-one edge','Add a new vertex'],'The remaining n−1 edges are original weight-one adjacencies forming a spanning path.'),
('If an accepted tour has zero weight-two edges, how is an original Hamiltonian path obtained?','Delete any one cycle edge',['No path can be obtained','Duplicate every edge','Delete one vertex'],'Breaking an all-original Hamiltonian cycle at any edge leaves a Hamiltonian path.'),
('For n=5, can a constructed tour with two weight-two edges meet threshold6?','No; its cost is7',['Yes; its cost is6','Yes; its cost is5','No; its cost is10 necessarily'],'Cost=n+r=5+2=7 exceeds n+1=6.'),
('What construction time is sufficient using an adjacency matrix for n vertices?','O(n²)',['O(2ⁿ) necessarily','O(log n)','O(n!) necessarily'],'Assign weight1 or2 to each unordered vertex pair, a quadratic number of pairs.'),
('Why does this prove an iff rather than only the forward implication?','Every accepted target tour can be broken into an original HP as well as every HP being closable',['Complete target graphs always imply original HP','Only the forward path-to-tour step matters','Weights need not encode adjacency'],'The n+r argument establishes the reverse direction; completion alone would be unsound.'),
('Malfoid deletes an arbitrary edge when the only weight-two edge is elsewhere in the tour. What is wrong?','The remaining path may still contain a nonedge of the original graph',['Any deletion always gives an original path','All weight-two edges were original','The threshold guarantees no weight-two edge'],'When one weight-two edge exists, delete that edge specifically so every retained edge belongs to the original graph.')])

topic('N5',[
('What two obligations establish NP-completeness of a decision problem B?','B∈NP and every NP problem reduces to B',['B∈P and B has a slow implementation','B has a short name and a graph','B reduces to an easy problem only'],'Membership supplies efficient verification; reductions supply hardness.'),
('Which proof direction uses a known NP-complete source A?','A≤pB',['B≤pA only','A≤pA only','B≤pB only'],'An efficient target solver would then solve A.'),
('What is a SAT yes-certificate?','A satisfying truth assignment',['One failed assignment','The word false','An arbitrary circuit size'],'Evaluate the formula under the proposed assignment and accept exactly if it satisfies all requirements.'),
('Does one failed truth assignment prove a formula unsatisfiable?','No',['Yes','Only when it has three variables','Only if the assignment is all false'],'UNSAT requires every assignment to fail, not just the candidate tested.'),
('Why does reducing one known NP-complete A suffice for hardness once A≤pB is proved?','Every NP language reduces to A, and composition gives a reduction to B',['Only A belongs to NP','All NP languages equal A','The runtime of A is copied into B'],'Transitivity carries the universal hardness property from A to B.'),
('If B has a polynomial verifier but no hardness reduction has been supplied, what has been established?','B∈NP only',['B is NP-complete','B∉P','P=NP'],'NP membership by itself includes easy problems and does not establish completeness.'),
('If an NP-complete problem has a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm, what follows?','P=NP',['P and NP become disjoint','Only that one instance is easy','NP becomes empty'],'Every NP language reduces to that problem, yielding a polynomial decider for each.'),
('Why must the target be phrased as a decision problem for this definition?','P/NP completeness here classifies yes/no languages',['Optimisation problems never have algorithms','Thresholds always simplify the answer to yes','Decision problems have no inputs'],'One can discuss related optimisation hardness, but the formal membership proof concerns a specified decision language.'),
('A proposed reduction maps all instances to one fixed satisfiable formula. Why does it fail for a source with no instances?','It maps no to yes and violates iff preservation',['Its runtime is too fast','Its output is too small','It uses a Boolean constant'],'A constant yes output cannot preserve source no answers.'),
('What distinguishes verifying a supplied satisfying assignment from solving SAT by enumeration?','Verification checks one candidate; enumeration searches many candidates',['Verification ignores the formula','Enumeration is always polynomial','All candidates are satisfying'],'A polynomial check does not bound the number of candidates tried by a search.'),
('A correct reduction A≤pB is exponential-time to construct. Is NP-hardness via polynomial reductions established?','No; the construction must be polynomial',['Yes; correctness alone suffices','Yes if B is finite','Only when A is unsatisfiable'],'Runtime is a defining requirement of the reduction, separate from logical correctness.'),
('If B∈NP and known NP-complete A≤pB, what conclusion is justified?','B is NP-complete',['B is certainly outside P','B is undecidable','B∈P is proved'],'These are the two required obligations; separation from P would additionally require resolving P versus NP.'),
('Malfoid says “NP-complete means mathematically proved exponential.” What is the correction?','NP-completeness does not itself prove a superpolynomial deterministic lower bound',['Every NP-complete problem is linear','NP-complete problems have no algorithms','Every exponential program proves completeness'],'A polynomial algorithm for one would imply P=NP; that conditional is distinct from an established exponential lower bound.')])

topic('N6',[
('What choice does nearest-neighbour TSP make at each step?','Cheapest edge from the current vertex to an unvisited vertex',['Globally cheapest edge anywhere regardless of path','Most expensive unvisited neighbour','The vertex with largest label'],'The choice is local to the current tour endpoint.'),
('After every vertex has been visited, what edge must be included?','Return to the starting vertex',['An edge to a new unlisted vertex','No edge; a path is already a tour','Every unused edge'],'A TSP tour closes into a cycle; omitting closure understates cost.'),
('Why maintain a visited set?','To prevent revisiting vertices before final closure',['To sort profits','To change edge weights','To guarantee global optimality by itself'],'The heuristic must produce a tour visiting each vertex once.'),
('Does cheapest-next choice guarantee an optimal tour on every complete weighted graph?','No',['Yes','Only because the graph is complete','Yes if weights are positive'],'Local cheapness can force an expensive final connection.'),
('For AB=1,BC=1,AC=2,AD=2,BD=2,CD=100, starting A, what is the first nearest-neighbour move?','A→B',['A→C','A→D','A→A'],'AB=1 is uniquely cheaper than AC=AD=2.'),
('For that graph, what comes after A→B under nearest-neighbour?','B→C',['B→A','B→D','Stop at B'],'C is unvisited and BC=1 beats BD=2; A is already visited.'),
('What is the nearest-neighbour tour cost starting A on that graph?','104',['7','102','6'],'A→B→C→D→A costs1+1+100+2=104.'),
('What is the cost of feasible tour A→C→B→D→A on that graph?','7',['104','6','8'],'2+1+2+2=7, proving the greedy104 tour is not optimal.'),
('Does this graph satisfy triangle inequality?','No; CD=100>CB+BD=3',['Yes because all weights are positive','Yes because it is complete','No because every graph with four vertices fails'],'Completeness and nonnegative weights do not imply metric distances.'),
('What can the104-versus7 example validly prove?','Nearest-neighbour is not always optimal on general complete weighted graphs',['A specific approximation theorem for all metric instances','No heuristic can ever be optimal','Every starting vertex returns104'],'It is one nonmetric counterexample; conclusions must match its assumptions.'),
('With n vertices and a simple scan of all unvisited vertices at each step, what runtime follows?','O(n²)',['O(log n)','O(n!) necessarily','O(1)'],'There are n selections and at most n candidate distances checked per selection.'),
('Why must tie rules and the start vertex be specified for an exact heuristic trace?','Different allowed choices can yield different tours',['They never affect a tour','Every complete graph has distinct weights','Tie rules prove optimality'],'A deterministic trace needs enough information to fix each local choice.'),
('Malfoid reports cost102 for A→B→C→D on the example and calls it the tour cost. What is missing?','The D→A return edge of cost2',['The B→A edge of cost1','All six graph edges','A new starting vertex'],'The open path costs102; the closed tour costs104.')])

topic('N7',[
('How does shortest-link order candidate edges?','Globally by increasing weight',['By distance from the current endpoint only','By decreasing weight','By vertex name only'],'Unlike nearest-neighbour, it considers the cheapest remaining edge anywhere.'),
('Which degree limit applies before final closure?','No vertex degree may exceed two',['No vertex degree may exceed one','Every vertex must immediately have degree two','Degree is ignored'],'A Hamiltonian cycle gives each vertex exactly two incident selected edges.'),
('Which cycles must be rejected during construction?','Cycles formed before all vertices belong to the final tour',['Every cycle including the final closure','No cycles ever need checking','Only odd cycles'],'A premature closed subtour cannot later absorb remaining vertices without violating degree constraints.'),
('What distinguishes Kruskal’s objective?','It seeks a spanning tree',['It seeks a Hamiltonian cycle','It requires every vertex degree two','It chooses only edges from one current endpoint'],'Kruskal rejects all cycles and permits degrees above two.'),
('With sorted weights AB1,BC2,AC3,BD4,CD5,DE6,AE7,BE8,AD9,CE10, which first two edges are accepted?','AB and BC',['AB and AC','AC and BD','CD and DE'],'AB and BC pass degree and premature-cycle tests.'),
('After AB and BC are accepted, why reject AC?','It closes the premature A-B-C cycle',['It gives A degree three','It is the final spanning closure','It is absent from the complete graph'],'Only three of five vertices would be enclosed.'),
('After AB and BC are accepted and AC rejected, why reject BD?','B would have degree three',['It necessarily forms a cycle','D already has degree two','Its weight is negative'],'B already touches AB and BC; adding BD violates the degree cap even though D is outside the component.'),
('After subsequently accepting CD and DE, which edge closes the final tour?','AE',['AC','BD','CE'],'The spanning path is A-B-C-D-E; its two endpoints A,E can now be joined.'),
('What is the resulting tour cost in that weighted example?','21',['15','18','28'],'Accepted AB,BC,CD,DE,AE cost1+2+5+6+7=21.'),
('Why can union-find alone not enforce the whole shortest-link rule?','It detects components/cycles but not the degree cap',['It cannot store vertices','It always rejects disconnected edges','It automatically counts all vertex degrees'],'Degree counters are needed separately, and final closure requires a controlled exception to cycle rejection.'),
('With E edges, what standard sorting cost precedes the scan?','O(E log E)',['O(log E)','O(E²) necessarily','O(V!) necessarily'],'A comparison sort orders the complete candidate list.'),
('With suitable union-find plus degree counters, what scan cost can accompany sorting?','O(E α(V))',['O(E!)','O(log V) total','O(V²E²) necessarily'],'Near-constant amortised union-find operations per edge give the inverse-Ackermann factor; sorting is still accounted for.'),
('Malfoid copies Kruskal and rejects every cycle including final AE. What goes wrong for TSP construction?','The result remains an open spanning path, not a tour',['The degree cap is automatically fixed','The tour becomes optimal','A tree is already a Hamiltonian cycle'],'Shortest-link permits the final spanning closure; Kruskal’s all-cycle rejection serves a different objective.')])

topic('N8',[
('What restriction makes the fractional exchange argument unavailable in 0/1 knapsack?','Items are indivisible',['Weights must be irrational','Profits cannot be compared','Capacity is not bounded'],'A fraction needed for equal-weight replacement may be illegal.'),
('For (w,p)=(10,60),(20,100),(30,120), which item has highest density?','Item1',['Item2','Item3','All tie'],'Densities are6,5,4.'),
('With capacity50, can all three whole items be selected?','No; total weight60 exceeds50',['Yes; total profit is high','Yes; each individually fits','Only if sorted first'],'Individual feasibility does not imply joint feasibility.'),
('Does one counterexample show density greedy never gives an optimum?','No; it shows no universal guarantee',['Yes','Only if profits are integers','It proves every heuristic fails on every input'],'A heuristic can succeed on some instances and fail on others.'),
('Under descending-density 0/1 selection, taking each item if it fits, which items are selected in the example?','Items1 and2',['Items2 and3','Items1 and3','All three'],'Items1,2 use30 capacity; item3 weighs30 and cannot fit the remaining20.'),
('What profit does that density-greedy result achieve?','160',['220','240','180'],'60+100=160.'),
('What is the true 0/1 optimum for the same instance?','220',['160','240','280'],'Items2 and3 weigh50 and profit100+120=220.'),
('How does the fractional optimum240 differ from a valid 0/1 solution?','It uses only two-thirds of item3',['It uses every whole item','It excludes item2','It ignores capacity entirely'],'Fractional greedy takes weights10+20+20; the last20 is a fraction of the weight30 item.'),
('Why is profit180 from items1 and3 feasible but suboptimal?','Their weight40 fits, but items2 and3 give220 at weight50',['Their weight exceeds50','Item3 may never be selected','Profit180 is larger than220'],'Subset comparison independently shows the better feasible pair.'),
('If an item does not fit, what should a skip-and-continue heuristic do?','Leave capacity unchanged and examine later items',['Subtract its weight anyway','Stop even if later smaller items fit','Take an illegal fraction'],'Capacity changes only when an item is actually accepted; a later item may still fit.'),
('With n unsorted items, what cost fits density sorting plus one feasibility scan?','O(n log n)+O(n)',['O(2ⁿ) necessarily','O(log n) total','O(nC) always'],'This is the heuristic cost; exact capacity-indexed DP has a different state count.'),
('Why does exact O(nC) DP not make the binary-input problem automatically polynomial-time?','C is a numeric magnitude and may be exponential in its bit length',['DP is always approximate','The optimum cannot be verified','Capacity must equal n'],'Its pseudo-polynomial dependence is distinct from polynomial encoded-input time.'),
('Malfoid subtracts an overweight rejected item and obtains negative capacity. What invariant was broken?','Remaining capacity changes only for accepted feasible whole items',['Every item must be accepted','Negative capacity improves the objective','Sorting requires capacity to decrease at every step'],'Rejecting an item must preserve the current feasible partial selection and its capacity.')])
